{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The nocturnal UHI and its cross-city variation are fundamentally governed by sky view factor and street canyon geometry: dense urban canyons trap longwave radiation and obstruct radiative cooling to the sky, so cities with lower sky view factors and deeper canyons exhibit a significantly larger night-time temperature gap relative to open countryside.","counterconditions":["If a city is characterized by low-density, sprawling single-story suburban development with high sky view factors, radiative trapping would be minimal and fail to explain a significant nocturnal UHI gap.","If field measurements demonstrate that daytime surface material heat capacity and thermal mass storage dominate the delayed cooling curve over radiative geometry, the sky view factor mechanism would be superseded.","If anthropogenic heat emissions in a specific city are proven to be an order of magnitude larger than the radiative flux deficit, geometry alone cannot be the primary driver.","If high-rise cities in arid climates consistently show lower nocturnal UHI gaps than low-rise cities in humid climates, the model must be extended or discarded in favor of an evapotranspiration framework."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Urban building geometry creates a canyon effect where the effective sky view factor is reduced. At night, surfaces cool by emitting longwave radiation. In a city with deep street canyons, much of this emitted radiation is intercepted by opposing walls rather than escaping to the cold sky. This radiation is absorbed and re-emitted, trapping heat in the urban canopy layer. Because the countryside has an unobstructed sky view (approaching a factor of 1), it radiatively cools much faster after sunset. Therefore, the magnitude of the nocturnal UHI strongly depends on the three-dimensional built density and building height-to-width ratio of the city.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30b